Angles with sum $90^\circ$ are **complementary**. The two acute angles of a right-angled triangle are complementary, so:

- $\sin(90^\circ - \theta) = \cos\theta$, $\cos(90^\circ - \theta) = \sin\theta$;
- $\tan(90^\circ - \theta) = \cot\theta$, $\cot(90^\circ - \theta) = \tan\theta$;
- $\sec(90^\circ - \theta) = \operatorname{cosec}\theta$, $\operatorname{cosec}(90^\circ - \theta) = \sec\theta$.

*Example:* $\sin 25^\circ \div \cos 65^\circ = 1$, because $\cos 65^\circ = \sin 25^\circ$; and $\tan 40^\circ\tan 50^\circ = 1$.
